{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Three hundred cubits the length of the ark.</b> Scripture specified for us the measure of its length and its width and its height for two reasons. The first is to make known to us the greatness of the miracle, that the meager held the abundant, for there were large creatures there — elephants and re'emim. The second is to make known to us that the principal beginning of the waters of the Flood was on account of licentiousness, as it is written (Genesis 6:2) ״וַיִּרְאוּ בְנֵי הָאֱלֹהִים אֶת בְּנוֹת הָאָדָם״ and so forth, and as our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 108a) 'they sinned with abarb' — i.e., with many — 'and were punished with abarb,' i.e., with many. And the reason for this is that through licentiousness they profaned the sanctity of the name יָהּ which mediates between man and woman, and when the יוֹ״ד הֵ״א is separated from them, what remains is אֵשׁ וְאֵשׁ, fire and fire, as it is written (Job 31:12) ״כִּי אֵשׁ הִיא עַד אֲבַדּוֹן תֹּאכֵל״. And this is what our Sages of blessed memory said (Sanhedrin 108a): 'they sinned with boiling and were punished with boiling,' for they themselves kindled the fire.\n\nAnd likewise we find in this parashah the number fifteen in several places. For it is written (Genesis 7:20) ״חֲמֵשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה אַמָּה מִלְמַעְלָה גָּבְרוּ הַמָּיִם״, and likewise the waters prevailed one hundred and fifty days (Genesis 7:24), which is fifteen tens.\n\nAnd likewise the number of the length and width and height of the ark also points to the number fifteen, for three hundred cubits in length upon a width of fifty yields, in every area of a single cubit, fifteen thousand cubits per cubit. And its height was thirty cubits, and it had three decks — that is, ten cubits per deck — for it had ״תַּחְתִּיִּם שְׁנִיִּם וּשְׁלִשִׁים״ (Genesis 6:16), all of them equal; this yields one hundred fifty thousand cubits for each and every deck. Thus the fifteen thousand cubits in every cubit of area correspond to the fifteen cubits by which the waters prevailed above the mountains, and these one hundred fifty thousand [in each deck] correspond to the waters prevailing one hundred fifty days, which is fifteen tens.\n\nAnd all these occurrences of the number fifteen indicate that even though their sentence was sealed for robbery, nevertheless the principal beginning was on account of licentiousness and the profanation of the name יָהּ, which equals fifteen." "And likewise we find with Hezekiah, that he was punished at first for not engaging in procreation (Berakhot 10a), and the prophet said to him, ״צַו לְבֵיתְךָ כִּי מֵת אַתָּה וְלֹא תִחְיֶה״ (Isaiah 38:1). And our Sages of blessed memory interpreted there: ״כִּי מֵת אַתָּה״ — in this world; ״וְלֹא תִחְיֶה״ — in the world to come. And what is the reason for this great wrath, that for the sin of neglecting procreation he would be punished in this world and in the next? Rather, because it is written (Isaiah 26:4) ״כִּי בְיָהּ ה׳ צוּר עוֹלָמִים״, and from this we learn that this world was created with the הֵ״א and the world to come with the יוֹ״ד, and by not engaging in procreation he caused the name יָהּ to be withdrawn from man and woman — and it is as if he destroyed both worlds, whose existence depends on the name יָהּ, for the name would withdraw from all created beings and ascend on high. Therefore his sentence was to be banished from both worlds.\n\nAnd likewise the generation of the Flood, because they profaned the name יָהּ, their sentence too was decreed to be banished from this world and the world to come, as is stated in chapter Chelek (Sanhedrin 107a) that the generation of the Flood has no share in the world to come, and so forth.\n\nTherefore its opposite is stated (Psalms 118:17) ״לֹא אָמוּת כִּי אֶחְיֶה״ — 'I shall not die' in this world, 'for I shall live' also in the world to come, ״וַאֲסַפֵּר מַעֲשֵׂה יָהּ״, for ״כִּי בְיָהּ ה׳ צוּר עוֹלָמִים״ — for both worlds are the making of יָהּ.\n\nTherefore, when Hezekiah repented and undertook to engage in procreation and restored the name יָהּ to its place, it was therefore said to him (Isaiah 38:5) ״הִנְנִי יוֹסִיף עַל יָמֶיךָ חֲמֵשׁ עֶשְׂרֵה שָׁנָה״ — corresponding to the name יָהּ. And the word ״יוֹסִיף״ is grammatically irregular, for it should have said ״הִנְנִי מוֹסִיף״; but this is because ״הִנְנִי יוֹסִיף״ — the initial letters form י״ה, to indicate that in the merit of the name יָהּ he merited this addition." "And this is what is concluded in tractate Sukkah, chapter HaChalil (53a): when David dug the shafts, the deep sought to flood the world, and so forth, until David said fifteen Songs of Ascents and raised it up. For David knew that the generation of the Flood was punished with the great deep on account of the sin of licentiousness, for the waters of the deep are hidden waters, and Solomon called licentiousness (Proverbs 5:15) ״מַיִם גְּנוּבִים״, which is to say, hidden — the opposite of what is said (Proverbs 9:17) ״שְׁתֵה מַיִם מִבּוֹרֶךָ״ and so forth. Therefore, when David needed to bring up the deep, he was afraid lest by raising it the world would be flooded just as happened to the generation of the Flood; therefore he said fifteen Songs of Ascents, to allude that in the merit of Israel being fenced from immorality and the name יָהּ mediating between them, they would be saved from the great deep.\n\nAnd this is why, among those fifteen Songs of Ascents, it is concluded (Psalms 128:3) ״אֶשְׁתְּךָ כְּגֶפֶן פֹּרִיָּה״ and so forth. He said ״אֶשְׁתְּךָ״ — the alef is pointed with a segol, to indicate that he is speaking of the time when there are three partners in a person: father and mother and the Holy One, blessed be He. And when the Holy One, blessed be He, is a partner in the matter, if so the name יָהּ mediates between them; therefore ״פֹּרִיָּה״ is written with the addition of יִ״ה.\n\nAnd likewise our Sages of blessed memory said (Sukkah 51b) that between the men's gallery and the women's gallery there were fifteen steps. And all this indicates that the number fifteen always mediates between man and woman, for all of this derives from the sanctity of the name יָהּ.\n\nTherefore all these measurements of the ark were always close to the number fifteen, as has been said."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 6, :notes {2 "The reading 'ברבה קלקלו וברבה נדונו' literally means 'with many they sinned and with many they were punished,' the Aramaic word 'רבה/ברבה' connoting abundance or multitude; I retained the untranslated technical term to preserve the wordplay with the variant 'ברותחין' ('with boiling')."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:27:46.546202569Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}